      "id": "sevt_01UPvgPBL6fFhFBZF3PYuRvG",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "## Result: unusable \u2014 no payload received\n\n**What happened.** I spawned the roster's payload agent (`clevin-swarm-F-payload-20260828T023054Z-dee9a3`, thread `sthr_01KfBT8uDQkCqdrFz3AsQCF8`) with the task \"Produce your payload.\" The thread came back with no content. The full notification I received, verbatim:\n\n```\n[child sthr_01KfBT8uDQkCqdrFz3AsQCF8 completed but produced no text output]\n```\n\n**Measurement.** There was nothing to measure. I wrote the received reply \u2014 an empty string \u2014 to `/workspace/child_reply.txt` and ran `wc -c`, which returned `0`. So step 2 completed, but on an empty input, not on a payload.\n\n**Truncation.** I want to be precise here rather than helpful-sounding: I cannot report that the reply was truncated, because I did not receive a reply that was then cut short. I received zero characters. Those are different failure modes and the experiment should not record them as the same thing. An oversize-truncation result would look like a large-but-clipped body, possibly with a size notice attached; what I got was a completion with no text output at all.\n\n**Platform notice about size.** None. The only platform notice in this run is the \"no text output\" line quoted above, and it says nothing about size, limits, or truncation. I am not going to reinterpret it as a size notice \u2014 that would be inventing evidence for the hypothesis exp7 is testing.\n\n**Caveat on the exp7 premise.** Since this is an oversize test, a plausible reading is that the payload was so large it was dropped upstream and surfaced as empty. That is a hypothesis, not something I observed. Nothing in what reached me distinguishes \"oversize payload silently dropped\" from \"agent produced nothing.\" If you want to separate those, a useful next step is to re-run asking the payload agent to first report its intended payload size as an integer and emit only a short prefix, so the size claim survives even if the body does not.\n\n**`first_40_chars` / `last_40_chars`** are empty strings below, since the file has zero bytes. I did not substitute placeholder text.\n\n```json\n{\"received_chars\": 0, \"truncated\": false, \"platform_notice\": \"none\", \"first_40_chars\": \"\", \"last_40_chars\": \"\", \"delegation_error\": \"[child sthr_01KfBT8uDQkCqdrFz3AsQCF8 completed but produced no text output]\"}\n```\n\nI put the \"no text output\" line in `delegation_error` rather than `platform_notice` because it describes the delegation outcome, not a size constraint. Say the word if your schema wants it in the other field and I'll re-emit.",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ],
      "processed_at": "2026-08-28T02:31:22.753045Z",
